---
description: frontend automated testing (E2E/L1 + unit layout)
alwaysApply: false
---
Use when writing frontend automated tests. Pair with `testing.mdc` for level selection and `stack-frontend-react` for runners. Pair with `monorepo-architecture` when E2E runs against in-repo backends.

### Tooling

- E2E, L1, and unit runners: see `stack-frontend-react` (examples: Playwright; Vitest + RTL — confirm there).
- Run locally via terminal (project package scripts, or the commands on the README) as well as in CI — see `testing.mdc` Local runs.

### Layout (frontend package root)

```text
e2e/                         # E2E and L1 specs
├── playwright.config.ts     # example filename — confirm the runner in stack-frontend-react
├── fixtures/                # Optional: auth/storage helpers
└── ...
src/
└── **/__tests__/            # Or colocated *.test.ts(x)
```

### E2E

- Assert user-visible outcomes (roles, labels, text, navigation, results shown in the UI).
- Do not assert implementation details (specific HTML tags, CSS class soup, internal component structure).
- Frontend-only apps: no backend/seed setup required.
- Monorepo: point the E2E runner at the test stack (real backends + isolated data) — see `monorepo-architecture` and `testing.mdc`.

### Integration L1

- Same E2E driver as E2E.
- Mock at the **network/service boundary** (example: Playwright route mocking — confirm the E2E runner in `stack-frontend-react`) **only** for third parties that cannot be isolated in the test env.
- Keep the app and first-party backends real when they are part of the monorepo test stack.

### Unit

- Prefer testing single-job domain/helpers and presentational behavior with clear contracts (see `testing.mdc` — leaves, not orchestrating containers).
- Query by role, label, or text. Avoid asserting internal React state or child component structure.
- Do not unit-test multi-step container orchestration with heavy mocks of every collaborator — cover that with E2E/L1 (or L2 on the backend).
- Align the unit runner with the app bundler (same resolve aliases, etc.) so imports match production.
